@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        You have shown yourself to me, O Christ, face to face. I meet you in your <br /> sacraments.
    </span>
    <span>Saint Ambrose, Defense of the Prophet David</span>
</div>

<ol>
    <li>
        Sacraments are the means by which God communicates, upholds, and actually bestows the promises 
        of His covenant upon His people throughout all ages. 
    </li>
    <li>
        Just as Christ is one person in two natures, the divine and the human, so too are Christ's sacraments
        one promise in two elements, the earthly sign and that which is signified in heaven.
    </li>
    <li>
        In baptism, the sign is water and the signified is the Holy Spirit. In the Lord's supper, the sign
        is bread and wine and the signified is union with Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        In a sacrament, the sign and the signified are bound together by the unchangeable promises of
        God, so that as surely as we see the sign, so too do we partake of the signified. In this way heaven
        and earth touch in the sacraments, and the great power of heaven is set before our eyes
        for all to behold.
    </li>
    <li>
        We do not confuse or mix the sign and the signified, as if the bread transforms into Christ or
        the pouring of water is the Holy Spirit. Rather, we distinguish the two, and attribute
        that which is earthly to the sign, and that which is heavenly to the signified.
        Therefore the sacraments are salvific according to the body of Christ and the Holy Spirit,
        not according to the bread and the water.
    </li>
    <li>
        But neither do we separate the sign and the signified, as if there is "Spirit baptism" and
        a "water baptism", or a "bread eucharist" and a "Christ eucharist". The promises of God have
        made the earthly and the heavenly elements inseparable, so that when we speak of a sacrament we refer
        to the one promise instead of the individual elements. As Saint Paul instructs,
        there "is one Lord, one faith, one baptism."
    </li>
    <li>
        Because the sacraments are one, it is proper to attribute to them the workings of both the
        sign and the signified, both the earthly and the heavenly elements.
        In baptism the Holy Spirit works to save a person, so we
        may rightly say that baptism saves. Likewise we receive Christ by faith when we partake of
        the Lord's Supper, so we may rightly say that we feast on Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        Like all other saving graces, the heavenly reality signified in the sacraments is received and made
        effectual by faith alone. If a person should unwisely partake of a sacrament without faith, they
        do not partake of the signified reality and instead receive only the sign unto judgement.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />